-- Q11281: Government Regulation of Business [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Horizontal price-fixing agreements among competing sellers","Price discrimination in commodity sales to competing buyers","Tying arrangements conditioning one product sale on another","Monopolization through exclusive dealing with select retailers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): The Robinson-Patman Act (1936) amends the Clayton Act and specifically prohibits price discrimination in the sale of commodities of like grade and quality where the effect may substantially lessen competition. Summit''s differing discounts for identical products to competing buyers is the classic scenario targeted by this statute.
Wrong (A): Horizontal price-fixing is a per se violation under Section 1 of the Sherman Act, not the Robinson-Patman Act. It involves agreements among competitors, not a single seller''s pricing practices.
Wrong (C): Tying arrangements are addressed under Section 3 of the Clayton Act, involving conditioning one product''s sale on the buyer purchasing a separate product.
Wrong (D): Exclusive dealing contracts are governed by the Clayton Act Section 3, not the Robinson-Patman Act, and involve restricting a buyer from purchasing from competitors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11281;

-- Q11296: Government Regulation of Business [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A tying arrangement, because one product''s sale is conditioned on purchasing another","Price discrimination, because competing providers cannot match the bundled pricing","A group boycott, because Dominion effectively excludes competing storage vendors","Predatory pricing, because Dominion sets prices below cost to eliminate rival providers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 3 of the Clayton Act, a tying arrangement occurs when a seller conditions the sale of one product (the tying product) on the buyer''s purchase of a separate product (the tied product). Dominion''s requirement that customers buy its storage service with the accounting platform is a classic tie.
Wrong (B): Price discrimination under the Robinson-Patman Act involves charging different buyers different prices for the same product, not conditioning purchases on buying additional products.
Wrong (C): A group boycott involves coordinated action by multiple firms to exclude a competitor, not a single seller bundling its own products.
Wrong (D): Predatory pricing involves selling below cost to drive out competitors. The facts describe a bundling requirement, not below-cost pricing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11296;

