-- Q64: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Statute of Frauds (Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 110), certain categories of contracts must be in writing to be enforceable: real property transfers, contracts not performable within one year, goods of $500+ under UCC Section 2-201, suretyship, and marriage consideration contracts. Henderson''s land sale falls within the real property category. Torres''s painting contract is performable within two weeks and involves services, so it falls outside every Statute of Frauds category despite its $8,000 value.
Wrong (A): While the legal analysis of each contract is correct, this choice accurately describes the Statute of Frauds distinction rather than stating the statute was abolished.
Wrong (B): The Statute of Frauds does not impose a blanket $5,000 threshold on all contracts. It applies to specific categories regardless of dollar amount, except UCC goods contracts at $500+.
Wrong (D): The $500 UCC threshold applies only to goods contracts, not services. The land sale is unenforceable without writing, not the painting contract.$EXPL$
